  "starter_code": "def merge_sorted_array(nums1: list[int], m: int, nums2: list[int], n: int) -> None:\n    # Write your solution here.\n    pass\n",
  "reference_solution": "def merge_sorted_array(nums1, m, nums2, n):\n    left, right, write = m - 1, n - 1, m + n - 1\n    while right >= 0:\n        if left >= 0 and nums1[left] > nums2[right]:\n            nums1[write] = nums1[left]\n            left -= 1\n        else:\n            nums1[write] = nums2[right]\n            right -= 1\n        write -= 1\n",
  "brute_force_solution": null,
  "known_wrong_solutions": [
    "def merge_sorted_array(nums1, m, nums2, n):\n    nums1[:] = sorted(nums1 + nums2)\n",
    "def merge_sorted_array(nums1, m, nums2, n):\n    return sorted(nums1[:m] + nums2)\n"
  ],
  "target_time_complexity": "O(m+n)",
  "target_space_complexity": "O(1)",
  "estimated_minutes": 20,
  "rubric": {
    "algorithmic_insight": 1,
    "prerequisites": 1,
    "implementation": 1,
    "performance_pressure": 0,
    "edge_cases": 0
  },
  "hints": [
    "Writing from the front risks overwriting unread nums1 values; the tail is free storage.",
    "Merge backwards from the largest remaining values of the two inputs.",
    "Compare nums1[m-1] with nums2[n-1], write the larger at the final free slot, and move the corresponding pointer; continue until nums2 is exhausted.",
    "Remaining nums1 values are already in place. Remaining nums2 values still need copying, including when m starts at zero."
  ],
  "editorial": "Use read pointers at the final real entry of each array and a write pointer at the end of nums1. The larger unread value belongs in the write slot. Moving backwards prevents overwriting unmerged nums1 entries. Once nums2 is exhausted, any remaining nums1 prefix already occupies its correct positions. The oracle sorts only the two real input prefixes. This differs from the original return-a-new-list merge exercise because mutation of caller-owned storage is graded.",
